@page "/scroll"

<PageTitle>Scroll · Bmotion samples</PageTitle>

<h1>Scroll</h1>

<p>
    The bar along the top of every page is the page's scroll, played by the browser on its own scroll timeline
    (<code>Timeline="BmScrollTimeline.Page()"</code>). Its keyframes are worked out in C# once and handed over; after
    that there is no scroll handler and no interop, so it follows the scroll as closely on Server and in the MAUI app as
    on WebAssembly. Scroll down.
</p>

<ScrollReadout />

<section>
    <h2>Each card's own journey</h2>
    <p>
        <code>BmScrollTimeline.View()</code> measures the element's journey through the viewport instead of the page's:
        0 as it enters at the bottom, 1 as it leaves at the top. These cards fade and grow in on the way up, and back
        out as they go. Only transforms and opacity can ride a scroll timeline - they are what the browser
        interpolates by itself.
    </p>

    <div class="journey">
        @foreach (var card in Enumerable.Range(1, 5))
        {
            <Bmotion Timeline="BmScrollTimeline.View()" Animate="Bm.To(opacity: [0, 1, 1, 0], scale: [0.85, 1, 1, 0.85])">
                <div class="journey-card">Card @card</div>
            </Bmotion>
        }
    </div>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Triggered by the viewport</h2>
    <p>
        <code>WhileInView</code> is a gesture rather than a timeline: the browser reports the element entering the
        viewport, and it plays an ordinary animation from there. <code>BmPresets.Reveal()</code> packages the usual one,
        once per element.
    </p>

    <ul class="reveals">
        @foreach (var (index, text) in Reveals.Index())
        {
            <Bmotion @key="index" Preset="index % 2 == 0 ? RevealFromLeft : RevealFromRight">
                <li class="reveal">@text</li>
            </Bmotion>
        }
    </ul>
</section>
